{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgg5ySQzLpwMkSpY4kps3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For [Co(ox)₃]³⁻, Dq ≈ 1856 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 432 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 1946 cm⁻¹. These come from the usual strong-field formulas for the transitions of low-spin d⁶.\n\n**Assumptions**\n- [Co(ox)₃]³⁻ is low-spin Co(III), d⁶, with ground state ¹A₁g (t₂g⁶).\n- Both bands are spin-allowed transitions from t₂g⁶ to t₂g⁵e_g¹.\n- In the strong-field limit, the energies of these transitions are:\n  - ¹A₁g → ¹T₁g: E₁ = 10Dq − C\n  - ¹A₁g → ¹T₂g: E₂ = 10Dq + 16B − C\n- Configuration-interaction corrections are ignored, as is standard for this textbook method.\n\n**Step 1 – find B from the gap between the two bands**\n\nE₂ − E₁ = 16B\n23 529 − 16 611 = 6 918 cm⁻¹\nB = 6 918 / 16 ≈ 432 cm⁻¹\n\n**Step 2 – find C from the given ratio**\n\nC = 4.5 × B = 4.5 × 432.4 ≈ 1 946 cm⁻¹\n\n**Step 3 – find Dq from the ¹T₁g band**\n\n10Dq = E₁ + C = 16 611 + 1 946 ≈ 18 557 cm⁻¹\nDq ≈ 1 856 cm⁻¹\n\n**Check**\n\n10Dq + 16B − C = 18 557 + 6 918 − 1 946 = 23 529 cm⁻¹, which matches the second band.\n\nB is about 39 % of the free-ion value for Co³⁺, which is roughly 1 100 cm⁻¹. That gives a nephelauxetic ratio β ≈ 0.39.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1856, \"B\": 432, \"C\": 1946, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Used strong-field low-spin d6 formulas E(1T1g)=10Dq-C and E(1T2g)=10Dq+16B-C with C=4.5B.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1673,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1673,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50d13d87-b222-40b2-9eeb-719d44b24ee0","uuid":"338dcfd9-48a4-4150-9412-c2c46f5990a8","timestamp":"2026-11-03T13:38:25.25